<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jebel Barkal</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Gebel Barkal</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Jebel Barkal is a flat-topped sandstone mountain near Karima in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sudan">Sudan</a>, held sacred as a home of the god <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amun">Amun</a>. Egyptian kings of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-kingdom-of-egypt">New Kingdom</a> built a temple at its foot, and the Kushite kings of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napata">Napata</a> enlarged it as the religious centre of their state; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piye">Piye</a> set up his victory stela there. It is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jebel-barkal.jpg" alt="Jebel Barkal" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: LassiHU · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gebel_Barkal.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>A new Kushite state took shape at Napata, near the holy mountain of <b>Jebel Barkal</b>, after Egyptian control of Nubia collapsed with the end of the New Kingdom around 1070 BCE and local elites reasserted independence.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kushite-empire">Kushite Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Some scholars believe the people of Kerma held an animist view of the world, pointing to their treatment of <b>Jebel Barkal</b> as a sacred place, a tradition later taken up by the Egyptians of the New Kingdom, who built a great temple of Amun there.</blockquote><a href="/culture/kerma-culture">Kerma culture · Beliefs &amp; Worldview</a></li><li><blockquote>Pharaohs built temple towns at Soleb, Sesebi, <b>Gebel Barkal</b> and Abu Simbel.</blockquote><a href="/state/viceroyalty-of-kush">Viceroyalty of Kush</a></li></ul></section>
